<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna `declorder`: a function with ten or more locals declares them in
    character order, so `v10` and `v11` print between `v1` and `v2`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P9 "naming-policy".  The P0 assertion
    `option declorder natural|name` decides the printed order of the local
    declarations; `natural` (the default) compares the digit runs of two names
    as numbers, `name` keeps the plain string sort.

    The fixture, gcc 11 -O0 (x86-64 SysV), keeps eleven call results live to
    the final expression so each is its own local:

        static long sink(long x) { return x * 5 + 1; }
        long tally12(long a)
        {
            long c1 = sink(a);
            long c2 = sink(c1);
            ...
            long c12 = sink(c11);
            return c1 ^ c2 ^ ... ^ c12;
        }

    Pass 1 runs with no option line, so it pins the shipped default: `v1`
    through `v11` in numeric order.  Pass 2 (`option declorder name`)
    reproduces the bug: `v10` and `v11` are declared between `v1` and `v2`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="sink"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x10001c" name="tally12"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu tally12</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option declorder name</com>
  <com>lo fu tally12</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="declorder #1: with no option line (the shipped default) v2 follows v1" min="1" max="1">^  uint8 v1; //
^  uint8 v2; //</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="declorder #2: and v10 follows v9" min="1" max="1">^  uint8 v9; //
^  uint8 v10; //</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="declorder #3: and v11 is the last declaration before the body" min="1" max="1">^  uint8 v11; //
^ *$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="declorder #4: with the option set to name, v10 is declared right after v1 -- the bug" min="1" max="1">^  uint8 v1; //
^  uint8 v10; //</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="declorder #5: and v2 right after v11" min="1" max="1">^  uint8 v11; //
^  uint8 v2; //</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="declorder #6: both passes declare the same eleven locals" min="22" max="22">^  uint8 v[0-9]+; // rax \| stack - 0x[0-9a-f]+$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="declorder #7: and print the same body" min="2" max="2">return v1 \^ v2 \^ v3 \^ v4 \^ v5 \^ v6 \^ v7 \^ v8 \^ v9 \^ v10 \^ v11 \^ sink\(v11\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
